  "content": {
    "id": "p21-question-59-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Given $m=\\dfrac{\\log(a)}{\\log(x)}$, show that $x^m=a$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^m=x^{\\log_x(a)}=a$.\n\nUse change of base to write $m=\\log_x(a)$, then use the inverse logarithmic identity.",
      "worked": "For $a>0$, $x>0$ and $x\\ne1$, change of base gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{\\log(a)}{\\log(x)}\\\\m&=\\log_x(a)\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^m&=x^{\\log_x(a)}\\\\x^m&=a\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
